```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE HARNESS_APP.ARCHIVE_OLD_ORDERS (
  P_CUTOFF_DATE DATE
) AS
  TYPE T_ROWS IS TABLE OF HARNESS_APP.ORDERS%ROWTYPE;
  L_ROWS      T_ROWS;

  CURSOR C_OLD_ORDERS IS
    SELECT ORDER_ID,
           CUSTOMER_ID,
           ORDER_DATE,
           STATUS,
           TOTAL
      FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
     WHERE ORDER_DATE < P_CUTOFF_DATE;
BEGIN
  OPEN C_OLD_ORDERS;

  LOOP
    FETCH C_OLD_ORDERS BULK COLLECT INTO L_ROWS LIMIT 10000;
    EXIT WHEN L_ROWS.COUNT = 0;

    FORALL I IN 1 .. L_ROWS.COUNT
      INSERT INTO HARNESS_APP.ORDERS_ARCHIVE (
        ORDER_ID,
        CUSTOMER_ID,
        ORDER_DATE,
        STATUS,
        TOTAL
      ) VALUES (
        L_ROWS(I).ORDER_ID,
        L_ROWS(I).CUSTOMER_ID,
        L_ROWS(I).ORDER_DATE,
        L_ROWS(I).STATUS,
        L_ROWS(I).TOTAL
      );

    FORALL I IN 1 .. L_ROWS.COUNT
      DELETE FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
       WHERE ORDER_ID = L_ROWS(I).ORDER_ID;
  END LOOP;

  CLOSE C_OLD_ORDERS;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    IF C_OLD_ORDERS%ISOPEN THEN
      CLOSE C_OLD_ORDERS;
    END IF;
    RAISE;
END ARCHIVE_OLD_ORDERS;
/
```

Assumptions:

- “Older than a given date” is implemented as `ORDER_DATE < P_CUTOFF_DATE`. If you want to include orders exactly at the cutoff, change it to `ORDER_DATE <= P_CUTOFF_DATE`.
- The procedure does not commit or roll back; the caller controls the transaction.
- `ORDERS_ARCHIVE` has the same listed columns as `ORDERS`: `ORDER_ID`, `CUSTOMER_ID`, `ORDER_DATE`, `STATUS`, and `TOTAL`.
- `ORDER_ID` is unique in `ORDERS`, so deleting by `ORDER_ID` identifies each fetched row.
- Batch order is not important for correctness because every matching row is archived and deleted. No tie-breaking rule is needed.

